1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Seven keys inserted in sorted order make a chain six levels deep, and a search for the largest key visits every one of them.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
The same seven keys, inserted as 10, 20, 30, 15, 25, 12, 5, sit in height 3. Finding 25 compares three keys.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
A red-black tree promises height at most twice the base-2 logarithm of n plus 1. For seven keys that ceiling is 6.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
An AVL tree promises 1.44 log base 2 of n plus 2. Red-black trees accept a taller tree in exchange for cheaper repairs.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Rule one: every node is red or black. One bit per node is the entire overhead of the structure.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Rules two and three: the root is black, and every leaf is black. The leaves here are the NIL markers, not keys.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Rule four: a red node has two black children. No path from the root may hold two reds in a row.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Rule five: every path from a node down to a NIL leaf holds the same number of black nodes. This is the rule that bounds the height.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
The black-height of a node counts the black nodes below it on the way to a NIL leaf, the NIL included and the node itself excluded.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
In this seven-key tree the root 20 has black-height 2, the red 12 also has 2, and every leaf key has 1.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Rule five says the two children of every node have equal black-height, and the numbers on screen satisfy it everywhere.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Insert 30 as a black node instead, and the two sides of 20 disagree: 1 against 2. Rule five breaks, rule four does not.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Claim: the subtree rooted at any node x holds at least 2 to the black-height of x, minus 1, keys.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Each child has black-height either bh of x or bh of x minus 1, so each subtree holds at least 2 to the bh minus 1, minus 1 keys.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Black-height 1 needs 1 key, 2 needs 3, 3 needs 7, 4 needs 15: at each step the smallest tree is a perfect all-black tree.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Turned around: n keys force a black-height of at most log base 2 of n plus 1, because 2 to the bh minus 1 cannot exceed n.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
No two reds in a row, so on any path at least half the nodes below the root are black, and the height is at most twice the black-height.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Chaining the two gives height at most 2 log base 2 of n plus 1. For fifteen keys that ceiling is 8.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
The ceiling is loose. This valid fifteen-key tree has height 5, and no fifteen-key red-black tree can be taller.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Height 6 needs at least 23 keys, so 5 is the largest height fifteen keys allow. The bound of 8 is never reached.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Insert as a red node. A red node adds no black to any path, so rule five survives by construction.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Insert 30 as black instead, and the paths through it hold one more black than the paths that miss it.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
A red insertion can only put red beside red. That is one broken rule, in one place, on one path.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
So the repair is about rule four: a red node whose parent is red, with an uncle on the other side of the grandparent.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Case one: the uncle is red. Recolour the parent and the uncle black and the grandparent red, and every path through the grandparent keeps its black count.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
The grandparent is now red, so the same fault can reappear one level up. The repair moves up, never down.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
On this ten-key build, inserting 6 fires case one at 4 and again at 7: two levels of climbing, no rotation at all.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Case one uses no rotation at all. Recolouring and rotating are different moves and are counted separately.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Case two: the uncle is black and the new red sits on the inside, a zig-zag. Rotate at the parent to straighten it.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Case three: uncle black, red on the outside. Recolour the parent black and the grandparent red, then rotate at the grandparent.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Inserting 12 into the five-key tree takes exactly that pair: rotate right at 15, then left at 10.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
One insertion needs at most two rotations, but the recolouring of case one can repeat at every level.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Deleting a red leaf changes no black-height and no adjacency, so the tree stays valid. 25 goes and nothing else changes.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Deleting a black leaf leaves one path short of a black node. Rule five breaks and rule four still holds.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
That is why deletion needs a fix-up of its own: the deficit is pushed up to the parent and repaired with a red sibling.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
The trade: an AVL deletion may rotate at every level, while a red-black deletion needs at most three rotations.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Step one: check the five rules on the drawn tree, and check rule five at every node, not only at the root.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Step two: for a counting question, use the black-height. At least 2 to the bh minus 1 keys, and height at most twice the bh.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Step three: for an insertion, insert red, then name the case from the uncle colour and the zig-zag.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Trap: a red-black tree is not an AVL tree. This valid red-black tree has a root balance factor of plus 2.

